<p>Smoke damage insurance claims in Dallas are often underestimated. Smoke and soot move through open doors, attic spaces, wall cavities, and HVAC ducts, settling on surfaces far from the fire. Odor soaks into porous materials and can linger for months. Because much of this damage is hard to see, it is easy for a claim to be scoped too narrowly.</p>
<p>This page explains how smoke damage happens, what a complete smoke claim usually includes, and how a public adjuster in Dallas can help. If your loss also involved flames or structural damage, see our <a href="/services/fire-damage-claims/">fire damage claims</a> page.</p>

<h2>Sources of Smoke Damage</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fires in your own building.</strong> Kitchen fires, electrical fires, and dryer fires can push smoke through an entire home even when the flames stay in one room.</li>
<li><strong>Fires in neighboring units.</strong> Apartments, condos, townhomes, and strip centers share walls, attics, and sometimes ventilation, so smoke crosses from one space to the next.</li>
<li><strong>Fires on nearby properties.</strong> A house fire next door or a commercial fire down the street can send smoke into homes and businesses.</li>
<li><strong>Puffback.</strong> A malfunctioning furnace or other fuel-burning appliance can release soot through the ductwork.</li>
<li><strong>Lightning-caused fires.</strong> NOAA has recorded several Dallas County house fires started by lightning, including fires in Carrollton and Farmers Branch in November 2025.</li>
</ul>

